            <fr:title text="Mosaic">Mosaic</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>The TPU kernel compiler <fr:link href="/060Z/" title="Pallas" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/060Z/" display-uri="060Z" type="local">Pallas</fr:link> lowers to — an <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link> pipeline over a <html:code>tpu</html:code> dialect, sitting where <fr:link href="/CQ6W/" title="Triton" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/CQ6W/" display-uri="CQ6W" type="local">Triton</fr:link> sits on GPU. A Pallas TPU kernel becomes a serialised Mosaic module inside a <html:code>@tpu_custom_call</html:code> attribute (<fr:link href="/QRDW/" title="A Pallas kernel is an opaque custom call" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/QRDW/" display-uri="QRDW" type="local">A <fr:link href="/060Z/" title="Pallas" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/060Z/" display-uri="060Z" type="local">Pallas</fr:link> kernel is an opaque <fr:link href="/M18X/" title="XLA custom call" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M18X/" display-uri="M18X" type="local">custom call</fr:link></fr:link>), which XLA then treats as a black box (<fr:link href="/2MM7/" title="A custom call is a black box XLA cannot fuse across" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/2MM7/" display-uri="2MM7" type="local">A <fr:link href="/M18X/" title="XLA custom call" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M18X/" display-uri="M18X" type="local">custom call</fr:link> is a black box <fr:link href="/M74N/" title="XLA Compilation" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M74N/" display-uri="M74N" type="local">XLA</fr:link> cannot fuse across</fr:link>).</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>What makes a Mosaic dump feel unlike a GPU one is that there are no threads in it</html:strong> (<fr:link href="/64A0/" title="Mosaic is explicit memory spaces and explicit copies" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/64A0/" display-uri="64A0" type="local"><fr:link href="/M3MH/" title="Mosaic" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/M3MH/" display-uri="M3MH" type="local">Mosaic</fr:link> is explicit memory spaces and explicit copies</fr:link>). A GPU IR is a thread and warp hierarchy in which data movement is implicit in which thread touches what. Mosaic is the opposite: explicit memory spaces on every <html:code>memref</html:code> (<fr:link href="/B1A1/" title="A memref names storage, a tensor is a value" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/B1A1/" display-uri="B1A1" type="local">A memref names storage, a tensor is a value</fr:link>) — hbm, <fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link>, smem — explicit DMA-style copies between them, semaphores for synchronisation, and vector ops on hardware tile shapes, typically <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[8 \times  128]]></fr:tex> for f32 (<fr:link href="/1XJ4/" title="Vectors are register-level, tensors are abstract" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/1XJ4/" display-uri="1XJ4" type="local">Vectors are register-level, tensors are abstract</fr:link>).</html:p>
            <html:p>That is the same shift <fr:link href="/060Z/" title="Pallas" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/060Z/" display-uri="060Z" type="local">Pallas</fr:link> asks of you at the source level, and the reason a TPU kernel is written as a pipeline of asynchronous copies rather than as an indexing scheme: the machine has no thread-level parallelism to hide a bad access pattern behind, so the copies have to be scheduled rather than merely correct.</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
