            <fr:title text="VMEM moves the ridge point"><fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link> moves the ridge point</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="anki">1786395976215</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link> vs <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> bandwidth: what is the ratio, and what does it do to the roofline?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  VMEM<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\to ]]></fr:tex>MXU bandwidth is roughly <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[22\times ]]></fr:tex> HBM bandwidth, so the <fr:link href="/KVM8/" title="Arithmetic intensity and the ridge point" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/KVM8/" display-uri="KVM8" type="local">critical arithmetic intensity</fr:link> for peak FLOPs drops from ~240 to <html:strong>~10–20</html:strong>.

  Worked, with <html:code>int8[16384,4096]</html:code> weights on v5e: FLOPs-bound at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B > 267]]></fr:tex> out of HBM, but only <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B > 11]]></fr:tex> out of VMEM — call it 20 in practice, since you cannot spend all of VMEM's bandwidth on weights.

  The consequence is <html:strong>VMEM prefetch</html:strong>: load the FFW weights during attention and the load disappears behind compute. The limit is capacity — VMEM is tiny, so a layer's weights have to be small or sharded enough to fit with room to spare.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
