            <fr:title text="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>A core component of the <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU architecture</fr:link> responsible for performing matrix multiplication via a weight stationary <fr:link href="/SXP6/" title="Systolic array" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/SXP6/" display-uri="SXP6" type="local">Systolic array</fr:link>.</html:p><html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> — 16,384 <html:em>multiply accumulate</html:em> units, each multiplying and adding in one cycle. v6e/Trillium widens the array to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex>, i.e. 65,536.</html:li>
  <html:li>The native op is one <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, or about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz. A v5e chip has enough MXUs to reach ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ul>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> 
The side length is fixed in hardware, so every dimension has to be tiled down to it and any axis shorter than it is padded up to it, wasting the FLOPs spent on the padding. The order is
<html:ol><html:li>Tiling the matrix to load from <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> to <fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link>.</html:li>
  <html:li>Compiler tiles from VMEM to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> tiles to load into the MXU.</html:li></html:ol>
</html:div>
<fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>10</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/HPSO/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>HPSO</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/HPSO/</fr:route><fr:title text="Why the MXU pads everything to 128">Why the MXU pads everything to 128</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786395976465</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What is the native op of one <fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:link>, and why does that force padding to 128?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  One <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, on a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> systolic array — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex> on v6e/Trillium.

  The array's side length is fixed in hardware, so any matrix axis shorter than 128 (256 on v6e) is <html:strong>padded</html:strong> up to it, and the FLOPs spent on the padding are simply wasted. With 2–4 MXUs per core, tiling dimensions want to exceed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times ]]></fr:tex> the MXU count.

  Scale: roughly <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz, so about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex> per v5e chip. int8 runs ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2\times ]]></fr:tex> faster, int4 ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[4\times ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



